  "articleBody": "When you're facing a roof aging assessment, having a clear paper trail can make a huge difference. Think of it as providing a medical history for your home's most important protective layer. This isn't just about showing us when the roof was put on, but also about understanding its journey—any repairs, major storms it's weathered, or even just routine check-ups. The more thoroughly you've documented your roof's life, the easier it is for us to get an accurate picture of its current state and future needs. It helps us understand if that curling shingle is just old age, or if it relates to a repair from years ago. A good history allows us to provide truly informed recommendations, avoiding guesswork and ensuring you get the most value from our services. We believe in honest communication, and that starts with good information from both sides.\n\n### What to Prepare Before You Start\n\n- [ ] **Original Build/Installation Date** — The exact date your roof was first installed, usually found in original home purchase documents or builder records. This provides the baseline for its chronological age.\n- [ ] **Permits for Roof Work** — Copies of any permits pulled for roof installation, major repairs, or additions. These often contain key details about materials and contractors.\n- [ ] **Past Repair Invoices** — Invoices or receipts from any previous roof repairs, detailing what was fixed, when, and by whom. This helps distinguish new damage from old.\n- [ ] **Maintenance Logs** — Any records of routine maintenance, cleaning, or professional inspections. Regular care can significantly extend a roof's lifespan.\n- [ ] **Warranty Information** — Documentation for any active material or labor warranties. These can influence decisions about repairs or replacement options.\n- [ ] **Storm Damage Dates/Reports** — Dates of any significant hailstorms or severe weather events that might have impacted your roof, along with any insurance claims filed. Texas weather can be brutal, and this context is vital.\n- [ ] **Attic Inspection Notes/Photos** — Any personal observations or photos of leaks, water stains, or visible damage from inside the attic. This can point to concealed issues.\n\n### How to use this checklist\nHaving these documents ready will help your roofing professional thoroughly assess your roof's aging and condition, providing you with the most accurate information to make decisions. Don't worry if you don't have every item; any information you have is valuable.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-02"
}